<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phaenomena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 275 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Phaenomena is a didactic poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-soli">Aratus of Soli</a>, written around 275 BCE at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a>. In about 1,150 hexameter lines it describes the constellations, the movements of the heavens and weather signs, putting into verse the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eudoxus-of-cnidus">Eudoxus of Cnidus</a>. It was immensely popular and was translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avienus">Avienus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phaenomena.jpg" alt="Phaenomena" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sebastian Ritter (Rise0011) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Streichholz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
